{"name":"Yale Blue","hex":"#0f4d92","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"yale","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mythical beast in European mythology and heraldry, usually portrayed as an antelope- or goatlike four-legged creature with large horns that it can swivel in any direction."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yale"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Yale Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Yale Blue is the dark blue color used in association with Yale University."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale_Blue","wikidata":"Q8047350"},{"title":"Yale University","blocks":[{"text":"Yale University is a private Ivy League research university in New Haven, Connecticut, United States. Founded in 1701, Yale is the third-oldest institution of higher education in the United States. It is one of the nine colonial colleges chartered before the American Revolution."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Yale_University_Shield_1.svg/330px-Yale_University_Shield_1.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale_University","wikidata":"Q49112"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/The_Victorious_Crew_of_1859.jpg/500px-The_Victorious_Crew_of_1859.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/The_Victorious_Crew_of_1859.jpg/1280px-The_Victorious_Crew_of_1859.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/The_Victorious_Crew_of_1859.jpg/500px-The_Victorious_Crew_of_1859.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The flag in this painting of the Yale 1859 crew team is believed to be the first documented Yale Blue, though the photograph is in black and white.[3]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Victorious_Crew_of_1859.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Yale_Bowl_scoreboard.jpg/500px-Yale_Bowl_scoreboard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Yale_Bowl_scoreboard.jpg/1280px-Yale_Bowl_scoreboard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Yale_Bowl_scoreboard.jpg/500px-Yale_Bowl_scoreboard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail